iral of slow turnaround and low flight rate. The most consequential decisions were requirements decisions made by institutions for non-technical reasons \u2014 and they were irreversible."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "Launch cost to orbit fell by roughly a factor of twenty in a single decade with no change in the underlying physics. Explain how: what stayed exactly the same, what actually changed, and why \"reusability\" alone is an insufficient explanation.", "rubric": "What stayed the same: every locked constraint. The rocket equation is identical (still exponential in Delta-v over exhaust velocity); the exhaust velocity is identical (kerolox, ~340 s, capped by chemistry); the ascent budget is identical (9.4 km/s, fixed by the planet); and the payload fraction of a reusable rocket is if anything WORSE than the expendable Saturn V's, because of the ~30-40% reusability tax. Not one physical number moved. What changed: three reinforcing ECONOMIC innovations \u2014 reusability (recover and re-fly the first stage, amortizing its cost across 20+ flights instead of discarding it), cheap manufacturing (vertical integration, mass-produced engines, no exotic materials, driving build cost down), and high flight rate (100+ flights a year, which both reuses each booster many times and spreads fixed costs thin). They form a virtuous cycle: cheaper flights raise demand, which raises flight rate, which improves amortization, which lowers cost further. Why reusability alone is insufficient: the Space Shuttle was also reusable and was the most expensive launcher ever (~54,500 dollars/kg), because it lacked cheap manufacturing and a high flight rate \u2014 ruinous to build and refurbish, and too complex to fly often enough to amortize its fixed costs. Reusability is a category holding both the best and worst answers; the revolution was the combination, none of it a physics change. The meta-lesson: when the physics is locked, the leverage moves to economics, which can move by orders of magnitude while the physics does not budge."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "State the unit's through-line principle and show how reusability is its largest application. Then explain the two enduring lessons the finale draws \u2014 about the word \"impossible\"/assumptions, and about where big projects really fail.", "rubric": "The through-line: never carry anything through a Delta-v (or a process) it does not need to experience without extracting its full value. It appears throughout the unit \u2014 staging jettisons empty tanks the instant they are dead; the gravity turn lets gravity do the steering instead of spending thrust on it; staged combustion routes the turbine exhaust into the main chamber instead of dumping it. Reusability is the largest application of all: the entire vehicle \u2014 a piece of aerospace perfection accelerated to the edge of space \u2014 was being carried through its own destruction, discarded after eight minutes as the ultimate dead weight. Recovering it applies the principle to the rocket itself, and it was hiding in plain sight for fifty years. First enduring lesson: expendability FELT as fundamental as the rocket equation, but it was a habit of mind, not a law of nature \u2014 a constraint everyone accepted that turned out to be a choice. The physics doors were genuinely locked; the price was never set by the physics, and the assumption that a rocket must be thrown away was simply wrong for fifty years. When a constraint is called fundamental, ask whether it is a law or a habit. Second enduring lesson: the Shuttle shows that the most consequential decisions in a large technical project are often made in the requirements phase, by institutions, for political or budgetary reasons, before engineers touch the hardware \u2014 and they are frequently irreversible. Brilliant execution cannot rescue a doomed requirement; the failure was in the committee room, not the wind tunnel. (Reusability also embodies a strategi